  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14268272/assets/6de7a204b3850900b14b3ec931f83fa5ba0f06951ab44e1e6a7936a95e941c96.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(2,6)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(6,2)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(2,4)$ and $(4,2).$\n\n- Plot II represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(6,2)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(2,6)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(4,6)$ and $(6,4).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
